{0 Grouped Product Encoding Plan}

This note records the conservative plan for reducing Why3 backend blow-up
without changing the semantic reference model. The explicit product remains
the source of truth until the grouped encoding is proved equivalent to it.

{1 1. Freeze The Current Backend}

Name the current proof backend [explicit-product]. It keeps the existing
construction:

- build the temporal automata;
- build the program/specification product;
- classify safe, bad-assumption, and bad-guarantee edges;
- generate the current edge-oriented Why3 obligations.

This backend is the reference used for comparison.

Status: implemented as [Pipeline_types.Explicit_product] and exposed through
[`--proof-encoding explicit-product`]. No grouped backend is accepted yet.

{1 2. Introduce A Product Obligation IR}

Introduce explicit intermediate records such as [product_edge_obligation] and
[product_obligation_group]. Each product edge keeps a stable identifier, source,
target, class, full guard, and associated program transition.

Groups are structural views over the same edges; they do not drop edges.

{1 3. Build Exact Grouped Formulas}

For each group, construct exact disjunctions of the original edge guards:

{[
safe_successor = guard_edge_1 \/ ... \/ guard_edge_n
bad_guarantee_successor = bad_guard_1 \/ ... \/ bad_guard_m
]}

The grouped formulas are a reformatting of explicit product edges, not a new
abstraction.

{1 4. Add A Flagged Backend}

Add a separate backend selection:

{[
--proof-encoding explicit-product
--proof-encoding grouped-product
]}

The grouped backend must remain opt-in until it has been validated.

{1 5. Check No Loss Of Edges}

Add static checks:

- the number of explicit edges equals the sum of grouped edge memberships;
- every explicit edge identifier appears exactly once;
- grouped disjunctions are built only from complete explicit edge guards.

These checks protect completeness during grouping.

{1 6. Compare On Small Examples}

Run both backends on small examples:

{[
explicit-product passes
grouped-product passes
edge coverage is identical
]}

If grouped obligations fail while explicit obligations pass, the problem is in
the Why3 encoding, not in the source specification.

{1 7. Measure Without Mixing Dump Costs}

Measure separately:

{[
--prove
--prove --dump-goals
--prove --dump-why3-vc
--prove --dump-smt2
]}

This separates proof time, VC/SMT generation time, and I/O cost.

{1 8. Improve The Why3 Shape}

If the grouped backend is correct but hard to prove, improve the generated Why3
shape using mechanically derived predicates or local lemmas. Do not add user
invariants merely to make proofs pass.

{1 9. Align Rocq After The Backend Works}

After the OCaml backend is validated, update the Rocq development with the
equivalence theorem:

{[
grouped relation = explicit relation obtained by edge disjunction
]}

Correction, progression, and completeness are then inherited from the explicit
product model.
